  "answer": "The vulnerability arises when the RRCReconfiguration message during handover is not integrity protected and ciphered, exposing it to tampering and eavesdropping. This violates the security requirements for RRC signaling, as RRCReconfiguration messages must be integrity protected and ciphered to prevent unauthorized modification or disclosure of configuration parameters. The source of the vulnerability is the potential misconfiguration or failure to enforce security activation before transmitting RRCReconfiguration during handover, particularly in inter-RAT scenarios where security may not be fully established prior to reconfiguration. This is especially critical in transitions involving E-UTRA/5GC or NR-DC, where security parameters must be properly derived and applied to ensure end-to-end protection of RRC signaling.",
  "evidence": "Clause 5.3.7.1 of 3GPP TS 38.331 states: 'The UE shall: ... 1 > if the RRCReconfiguration message was received as part of ConditionalReconfiguration: 4 > continue using the configuration used prior to the reception of RRCReconfiguration message; 3 > initiate the connection re-establishment procedure ...'. This implies that RRCReconfiguration is only processed after successful security activation. Furthermore, clause 5.3.7.2 specifies that 'The UE shall: ... 1 > configure lower layers to resume ciphering for SRB1 using the previously configured algorithm and the K key ...' only after integrity verification. Clause 5.3.7.3 confirms that 'The UE shall: ... 1 > if the RRCReconfiguration message was received over SRB1 or SRB3: ... 2 > if the RRCReconfiguration message was received as part of ConditionalReconfiguration: 4 > continue using the configuration used prior to the reception of RRCReconfiguration message' — indicating that RRCReconfiguration is not processed unless security is active. Additionally, clause 5.3.7.4 states that 'The UE shall: ... 1 > if the RRCReconfiguration message was received via NR (i.e., NR standalone, NE-DC, or NR-DC): ... 2 > if the UE is unable to comply with (part of) the configuration ... 3 > initiate the connection re-establishment procedure ...' — reinforcing that RRCReconfiguration must be integrity-protected and ciphered before processing. Thus, failure to ensure integrity protection and ciphering of RRCReconfiguration during handover

